<h2>Shapes and Play — design a park</h2>
<p><a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/0tfnu5q9bq0m7uwi76vg2oe1vcqq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original 2021 student tasks (3 pages)</a> show two park images, a design activity and original park problems. Publisher artwork remains source-linked only.</p>
<p>Make a grid park with FIVE of the original nine feature choices. One dot spacing horizontally/vertically is ONE YARD. State area in <strong>square yards</strong> and perimeter in <strong>yards</strong> for three features. Teacher checks your real design, measured shapes, and explanation.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Original source park problem</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>6-by-14-yard playground fencing</td><td>40 yards of fence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Same playground floor area</td><td>84 square yards</td></tr>
<tr><td>Different-area rectangle, same P40</td><td>8×12 yields area96 sq yd (one valid example)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two perimeters with A48 sq yd</td><td>4×12 gives P32 yd, 6×8 gives P28 yd (one valid pair)</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Open sketches, valid alternative dimension choices, photo Notice/Wonder, and partner discussions require teacher review; no single numeric claim substitutes for these source tasks. Original publisher Cool-down gated.</p>
</section>
